  "slug": "heat-stress-95b0",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a proteomics study examine plant stress memory, chromatin regulation, and hormone signaling under combined and osmotic stresses.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is how plants integrate multiple abiotic stresses at the level of chromatin and hormone signaling. In potato, combined heat and drought alter chromatin accessibility and prime ABA biosynthesis via StNCED3 on repeated exposure. In rice, thermopriming produces a restrained, lower-entropy heat transcriptional response with H3K27me3 retention and memory chaperone expression. In Arabidopsis, combined heat and phosphate starvation drives organ-specific jasmonate changes, with JA-Ile rising in roots and JA falling in shoots. A separate proteomics study links REVEILLE8-like clock genes to osmotic and salt stress responses, altering photosynthesis and phenylpropanoid pathways. What is new is the emphasis on priming and organ-level divergence under stress combinations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
